From 6ffa195b6483fe4d7ec8e1acfde7679897a03ec7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry Baryshkov <dmitry.baryshkov@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:39:20 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: spm: don't clobber the SAW control registers

The SAWv4.1 and SAWv2.3 register tables have no sequence entry, so
probe copies the all-zero sequence buffer to offset 0 instead, wiping
the SPM_CTL, SPM_DLY and SAW_CFG values the boot firmware programmed.
On MSM8998 the firmware then still powers the gold cluster down once
its last CPU goes offline, but never brings it back: PSCI CPU_ON leaves
the first CPU pending and the SoC hangs or resets.

Only write the sequence when the SAW has a sequence entry, like the
other registers.

Fixes: 13e72c3e2261 ("soc: qcom: spm: Implement support for SAWv4.1, SDM630/660 L2 AVS")
Signed-off-by: Dmitry Baryshkov <dmitry.baryshkov@oss.qualcomm.com>
Reviewed-by: Konrad Dybcio <konrad.dybcio@oss.qualcomm.com>
Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20261019-fix-qcom-saw-v1-2-9c7ba3fc2493@oss.qualcomm.com
Signed-off-by: Bjorn Andersson <andersson@kernel.org>

Backport this prerequisite to maintained Linux 6.18.63. The complete
seventeen-change batch passes native ARM, ARM64 and ARM64 RT object
builds with the MSM8926 feature enabled and disabled. CPU-idle is ARM
only. Host fault models use synthetic services and normal LeakSanitizer;
firmware, electrical and physical suspend acceptance remain open.

Codex prepared this local backport and verification. Original authors,
dates, licenses and human attribution tags are retained. Human DCO and
review remain pending. No phone access or publication occurred.

Backported from commit efa32ff01e4f ("soc: qcom: spm: don't clobber
the SAW control registers")
---
 drivers/soc/qcom/spm.c | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/qcom/spm.c b/drivers/soc/qcom/spm.c
index b983a27f7f0c5..0bfa99fde0d74 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/spm.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/spm.c
@@ -619,9 +619,11 @@ static int spm_dev_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return spm_8226_l2_register(pdev, drv);
 
 	/* Write the SPM sequences first.. */
-	addr = drv->reg_base + drv->reg_data->reg_offset[SPM_REG_SEQ_ENTRY];
-	__iowrite32_copy(addr, drv->reg_data->seq,
-			ARRAY_SIZE(drv->reg_data->seq) / 4);
+	if (drv->reg_data->reg_offset[SPM_REG_SEQ_ENTRY]) {
+		addr = drv->reg_base + drv->reg_data->reg_offset[SPM_REG_SEQ_ENTRY];
+		__iowrite32_copy(addr, drv->reg_data->seq,
+				 ARRAY_SIZE(drv->reg_data->seq) / 4);
+	}
 
 	/*
 	 * ..and then the control registers.
-- 
